CAT = "panceEndo"; SEG = "END"
Q = [
("A 44-year-old woman has fatigue, weight gain and constipation, a high TSH, a low free T4 and positive anti-TPO antibodies. What is the cause?",
 "Hashimoto thyroiditis", ["Graves disease", "Subacute thyroiditis", "Pituitary adenoma"],
 "Primary hypothyroidism with thyroid peroxidase antibodies is Hashimoto (chronic lymphocytic) thyroiditis, the most common cause in iodine-sufficient areas. It is treated with levothyroxine titrated to the TSH."),
("A 33-year-old has weight loss, tremor, proptosis and a low TSH. Which radioactive iodine uptake pattern fits the likely diagnosis?",
 "Diffuse increased uptake", ["Diffuse low uptake", "Single hot nodule", "Patchy mixed uptake"],
 "Graves disease, with TSH receptor antibodies, gives diffuse high uptake; eye disease and pretibial myxedema are specific to it. Low uptake suggests thyroiditis or exogenous hormone, and patchy uptake a toxic multinodular goiter."),
("Three weeks after a viral upper respiratory illness, a 38-year-old has a very tender thyroid, palpitations, a high ESR and low radioiodine uptake. What is the diagnosis?",
 "Subacute thyroiditis", ["Toxic multinodular goiter", "Anaplastic thyroid cancer", "Acute suppurative thyroiditis"],
 "Painful subacute (de Quervain) thyroiditis follows viral illness and releases stored hormone, so uptake is low. It is treated with NSAIDs and a beta blocker; antithyroid drugs do not help."),
("Which hemoglobin A1c, confirmed on repeat, meets the criterion for diabetes?",
 "6.5%", ["5.7%", "6.1%", "6.3%"],
 "An A1c of 6.5% or more diagnoses diabetes, as does a fasting glucose of 126 mg/dL or more or a 2-hour value of 200 mg/dL or more on an oral glucose tolerance test. An A1c of 5.7 to 6.4% is prediabetes."),
("A 56-year-old has hyponatremia with a low serum osmolality, a urine osmolality of 480 mOsm/kg, high urine sodium and normal volume status. Which diagnosis fits?",
 "SIADH", ["Central diabetes insipidus", "Primary polydipsia", "Hypovolemic hyponatremia"],
 "Euvolemic hypotonic hyponatremia with inappropriately concentrated urine and urine sodium above 30 mEq/L is SIADH once hypothyroidism and adrenal insufficiency are excluded. Primary polydipsia makes very dilute urine."),
("A 29-year-old has constant thirst and 7 liters a day of dilute urine. After water deprivation, urine osmolality rises sharply with desmopressin. Which diagnosis fits?",
 "Central diabetes insipidus", ["Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus", "Primary polydipsia", "Uncontrolled diabetes"],
 "A rise in urine osmolality after desmopressin shows the kidney can respond, so ADH production is missing: central diabetes insipidus. In nephrogenic DI desmopressin has little effect."),
("A 47-year-old has central obesity, purple striae, proximal weakness and new diabetes. Which is an appropriate first screening test?",
 "Dexamethasone suppression", ["Random serum cortisol level", "Cosyntropin stimulation", "Morning plasma ACTH level"],
 "Cushing syndrome is screened with an overnight 1 mg dexamethasone suppression test, late-night salivary cortisol or 24-hour urine free cortisol. Random cortisol varies too much, and cosyntropin stimulation looks for adrenal insufficiency."),
("A 39-year-old has fatigue, darkening skin, salt craving, a sodium of 128 and a potassium of 5.8 mEq/L. Which test confirms the likely diagnosis?",
 "Cosyntropin stimulation", ["Dexamethasone suppression", "Plasma renin activity", "Oral glucose tolerance"],
 "Hyperpigmentation, hyponatremia and hyperkalemia suggest primary adrenal insufficiency. A low cortisol response to cosyntropin confirms it, and a high ACTH shows the problem is in the adrenal gland."),
("A 42-year-old has episodes of pounding headache, sweating and palpitations with a BP of 210/120 during attacks. Which test is the best first step?",
 "Plasma free metanephrines", ["Serum aldosterone and renin", "Urine free cortisol", "Thyroid uptake scan"],
 "Episodic headache, sweating and tachycardia with paroxysmal hypertension suggest pheochromocytoma. Plasma free or 24-hour urine fractionated metanephrines are the first test, before any imaging."),
("A 50-year-old has resistant hypertension and a potassium of 3.1 mEq/L without diuretics. Which screening test is indicated?",
 "Aldosterone to renin ratio", ["Plasma free metanephrines", "Overnight dexamethasone", "Serum calcium and PTH"],
 "Hypertension with spontaneous hypokalemia suggests primary hyperaldosteronism, screened with a morning aldosterone to renin ratio. A high ratio is confirmed by a suppression test before adrenal imaging."),
("A 61-year-old with kidney stones and bone pain has a calcium of 11.4 mg/dL and a high PTH. What is the most likely cause?",
 "Parathyroid adenoma", ["Malignancy with PTHrP", "Vitamin D toxicity", "Milk-alkali syndrome"],
 "High calcium with a high or inappropriately normal PTH is primary hyperparathyroidism, most often from a single adenoma. In malignancy and vitamin D excess, PTH is suppressed."),
("A healthy 13-year-old boy is the shortest in his class, grows at a normal rate along the 3rd percentile and has a delayed bone age. Both parents had late puberty. What is the likely cause?",
 "Constitutional growth delay", ["Familial short stature", "Growth hormone deficiency", "Primary hypothyroidism"],
 "Constitutional delay gives normal growth velocity, a delayed bone age and late puberty, with normal final height. Familial short stature has a bone age that matches age, and hormone deficiencies slow growth velocity."),
]
